------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 2 — is Delta self-adjoint in the Weil inner product?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  test:  Q A = A^T Q   for each operator A
  measure: ||Q A - A^T Q|| / ||Q A||   (0 = self-adjoint)

                            operator            failure
  ---------------------------------- ------------------
           Delta (dyadic difference)         0.99844421
   d/dt (scaling gen) [control PASS]        0.038227574
        random matrix [control FAIL]         0.86374748

==============================================================================
READ THE RESULT
==============================================================================

  Delta's failure : 0.99844421
  scaling gen     : 0.038227574   <- the operator CvS is built around
  random          : 0.86374748   <- total failure baseline

  Delta near the scaling generator
      -> the Weil form does supply the inner product, and O1's negative was
         an artifact of restricting to diagonal weights.  The two programs
         are then the same operator in different coordinates.

  Delta near the random baseline
      -> the Weil form is not the missing ingredient for THIS operator.  The
         quantity above is then the size of the gap, which is the useful
         thing to report either way.

  Neither outcome bears on RH.  O2 separately closed the question of whether
  any operator built here has {gamma} as its spectrum.

  CAVEAT ON THE BASIS.  Q lives in the trigonometric basis e_k(t) on
  [0, log c].  Delta as written here differences the BASIS INDEX k, which is
  the natural finite-dimensional analogue but is not automatically the same
  as the dyadic shift on the underlying interval.  If the failure comes out
  large, the first thing to check is whether the basis alignment is right --
  not to conclude the operators are unrelated.

